 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "ai1",
   "source_id": "but how much greater?",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "interaction",
   "prompt": "<p>Two levels of an atom are 2.0 eV apart and have the same degeneracy, $g_1 = g_2$. In thermal equilibrium, find the ratio $N_2/N_1$ (a) at 300 K and (b) at 3000 K. Take $k_B = 8.617 \\times 10^{-5}$ eV/K.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 2.52e-34,
      "tol": 0.1,
      "hint": "Work out $k_BT$ in eV first, then $\\Delta E/k_BT$."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 0.000437,
      "tol": 0.05
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>With $g_1 = g_2$, $N_2/N_1 = e^{-\\Delta E/k_BT}$.</p><p>(a) $k_BT = (8.617 \\times 10^{-5}\\ \\text{eV/K})(300\\ \\text{K}) = 0.02585$ eV, so $\\Delta E/k_BT = 2.0/0.02585 = 77.4$ and $N_2/N_1 = e^{-77.4} = 2.5 \\times 10^{-34}$.</p><p>(b) $k_BT = 0.2585$ eV, $\\Delta E/k_BT = 7.74$ and $N_2/N_1 = e^{-7.74} = 4.4 \\times 10^{-4}$.</p><p>Even at 3000 K fewer than one atom in two thousand is in the upper level, so a photon at this frequency is far more likely to be absorbed than to stimulate emission.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "ai2",
   "source_id": "If we know one of the Einstein coefficients",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "relationship",
   "prompt": "<p>The red line of a helium-neon laser is at 632.8 nm. (a) Find $A_{21}/B_{21}$ for this transition. (b) If $A_{21} = 6.56 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\text{s}^{-1}$, find $B_{21}$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 6.57e-14,
      "unit": "J·s/m³",
      "hint": "Find $\\nu_{12} = c/\\lambda$ and use Equation 15.2.10."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 9.98e+19,
      "unit": "m³ J⁻¹ s⁻²"
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>(a) $\\nu_{12} = c/\\lambda = (2.998 \\times 10^{8}\\ \\text{m/s})/(632.8 \\times 10^{-9}\\ \\text{m}) = 4.738 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. From Equation 15.2.10, $A_{21}/B_{21} = 8\\pi h\\nu_{12}^3/c^3 = 8\\pi(6.626 \\times 10^{-34}\\ \\text{J·s})(4.738 \\times 10^{14}\\ \\text{s}^{-1})^3/(2.998 \\times 10^{8}\\ \\text{m/s})^3 = 6.57 \\times 10^{-14}$ J·s/m³.</p><p>(b) $B_{21} = A_{21}/(6.57 \\times 10^{-14}\\ \\text{J·s/m}^3) = (6.56 \\times 10^{6}\\ \\text{s}^{-1})/(6.57 \\times 10^{-14}\\ \\text{J·s/m}^3) = 9.98 \\times 10^{19}\\ \\text{m}^3\\,\\text{J}^{-1}\\,\\text{s}^{-2}$.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
